= Introduction <Introduction>
Extract-transform-load (ETL) workflows combine data from multiple sources, transform it, and load it into a data warehouse. `pygrametl` is a Python framework for implementing such workflows @pygrametl. Its parallel extension introduced task and data parallelism through `multiprocessing` in CPython and multithreading in Jython @pygrametlPara. In standard CPython, the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) prevents Python bytecode from executing in parallel across threads, so `pygrametl` uses separate processes at the cost of inter-process communication and additional memory. Jython avoids the GIL but is tied to Python 2.7 and has a substantially larger memory footprint. Independently of the Python runtime, row-by-row database operations can leave workers waiting for a reply after every statement.

Recent Python releases introduce alternatives to these trade-offs: asynchronous I/O, subinterpreters, and free-threaded CPython builds that can run Python threads without the GIL. In parallel, psycopg 3 exposes PostgreSQL pipeline mode, which can send multiple statements before waiting for their responses. These mechanisms address different potential bottlenecks: concurrency can increase work in flight or use more CPU cores, while pipelining can reduce idle time caused by database round trips.

This paper asks which of these approaches meaningfully improves `pygrametl`'s execution time, and what resource or reliability costs accompany that improvement. We compare subinterpreters with multiprocessing using standalone microbenchmarks, then evaluate asynchronous extraction, free-threading, and a pipelined connection wrapper with a star-schema ETL workload based on @Thomsen2011Exaprogs. All ETL variants are measured against the same sequential CPython baseline. This lets us distinguish gains from parallel execution from gains due to reducing database wait time, and identify which changes merit further development.